<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      No permissions, deliberately. Manifest merging is transitive: anything
      declared here lands in every host app, and none of it is needed to
      compress. Background compression and legacy saveToDownloads() are opt-in;
      the app declares what it wants. See the README.

      The <service> below is a declaration, not a permission — it has to be here
      for the class to be startable at all. It is inert unless the app both
      declares FOREGROUND_SERVICE *and* passes a VideoCompressConfig with
      `androidNotification`, because the plugin supplies no notification of its
      own.

      It is, however, VISIBLE. This element merges into the app's manifest, gets
      compiled into the APK's binary AndroidManifest.xml, and is readable through
      PackageManager — so APK inspectors such as LibChecker list
      `com.compress.all.flutter_compress_pro.CompressionService` and can fingerprint
      this plugin from it. To trace any merged element back to its origin, read
      `<app>/build/app/outputs/logs/manifest-merger-<variant>-report.txt`; each
      entry names the module that added it.

      Apps that never want background compression can drop it entirely, which
      also removes it from that component listing:

          <service android:name="com.compress.all.flutter_compress_pro.CompressionService"
              tools:node="remove" />
    -->
    <application>
        <service
            android:name=".CompressionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync">
            <!--
              Attribution, readable from the built APK.

              Unlike the XML comments above (which never reach the binary
              manifest), <meta-data> is compiled in and surfaces through
              PackageManager's GET_META_DATA — so anyone inspecting the APK with
              LibChecker or similar can see which package this component belongs
              to, instead of having to guess from the class name.
            -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="io.pub.package"
                android:value="flutter_compress_pro" />
            <meta-data
                android:name="io.pub.package.url"
                android:value="https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_compress_pro" />
        </service>
    </application>
</manifest>
